# Common .NET Problems — Troubleshooting Guide

This is one of the **most important practical sections** for a .NET developer. In real projects, knowing how to identify the problem is just as important as writing code.

Use this general troubleshooting flow:

```text
Problem
   ↓
Check HTTP Status / Error Message
   ↓
Check Visual Studio Output / Logs
   ↓
Check Exception
   ↓
Check Call Stack
   ↓
Identify Layer
   ↓
Fix
   ↓
Test Again
```

---

# 1. API Not Starting

Typical symptoms:

```text
Application failed to start
Unable to connect to localhost
Unable to bind to address
500 during startup
Process terminated
```

Check:

```text
Program.cs
Port
Configuration
Dependencies
Logs
```

---

## 1.1 Check Program.cs

First inspect:

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddControllers();

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseHttpsRedirection();

app.MapControllers();

app.Run();
```

Look for:

* Missing service registration
* Incorrect middleware
* Incorrect configuration
* Exception during application startup
* Incorrect dependency registration

For example:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<IEmployeeService, EmployeeService>();
```

If `EmployeeService` has dependencies that aren't registered correctly, the application can fail when resolving the service.

---

# 2. Port Problem

You may see:

```text
Failed to bind to address
Address already in use
```

Check the URL shown when the API starts:

```text
https://localhost:7001
http://localhost:5001
```

You can also check:

```text
Properties
   ↓
launchSettings.json
```

Example:

```json
{
  "profiles": {
    "https": {
      "applicationUrl": "https://localhost:7001;http://localhost:5001"
    }
  }
}
```

If another application is already using the port, change the port or stop the process using it.

---

# 3. Configuration Problem

Check:

```text
appsettings.json
appsettings.Development.json
Environment Variables
User Secrets
```

Example:

```json
{
  "ConnectionStrings": {
    "DefaultConnection": "Server=.;Database=EmployeeDB;Trusted_Connection=True;TrustServerCertificate=True;"
  }
}
```

Then:

```csharp
var connectionString =
    builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("DefaultConnection");
```

Check carefully:

```text
Key name
Database name
Server name
Environment
Value
```

---

# 4. Dependency Problem

Suppose you have:

```csharp
public EmployeesController(
    IEmployeeService employeeService)
{
    _employeeService = employeeService;
}
```

but forgot:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<IEmployeeService, EmployeeService>();
```

You may receive an error similar to:

```text
Unable to resolve service for type
'IEmployeeService'
while attempting to activate
'EmployeesController'
```

### Fix

Register the dependency:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<
    IEmployeeService,
    EmployeeService>();
```

