# 7. Database Connection Failure

Typical error:

```text
A network-related or instance-specific error occurred
while establishing a connection to SQL Server.
```

Check:

```text
Connection string
SQL Server
Credentials
Firewall
Database name
```

---

# 8. Check Connection String

Example:

```json
{
  "ConnectionStrings": {
    "DefaultConnection":
      "Server=localhost;Database=EmployeeDB;Trusted_Connection=True;TrustServerCertificate=True;"
  }
}
```

Check:

### Server

```text
localhost
.
localhost\SQLEXPRESS
SERVERNAME
```

depending on your SQL Server installation.

### Database

Make sure:

```text
EmployeeDB
```

actually exists.

### Authentication

If using SQL authentication:

```text
User ID
Password
```

must be correct.

If using Windows authentication:

```text
Trusted_Connection=True
```

or the equivalent supported configuration should match your setup.

---

# 9. Check SQL Server

Make sure SQL Server is running.

Check:

```text
SQL Server
   ↓
SQL Server Management Studio
```

Try connecting manually.

If SSMS cannot connect, your .NET API will normally not be able to connect either.

---

# 10. Check Database Name

Suppose your connection string says:

```text
Database=EmployeeDB
```

but the actual database is:

```text
EmployeeManagementDB
```

The connection/database operation can fail.

Verify in SQL Server:

```sql
SELECT name
FROM sys.databases;
```

---

# 11. Check Credentials

For SQL authentication:

```text
Server
Username
Password
Database
```

all need to be valid.

Don't put real production passwords directly into:

```text
appsettings.json
```

or Git.

Use secure configuration/secrets.

---

# 12. Firewall

For remote SQL Server connections, check:

```text
Client
   ↓
Network
   ↓
Firewall
   ↓
SQL Server
```

A firewall can block the SQL Server port.

For a local development setup, firewall is less commonly the first cause than an incorrect server/instance or SQL Server service configuration.

