# 14. 401 — Unauthorized

Remember:

```text
401 → Authentication problem
```

Usually means the API could not successfully authenticate the caller.

Check:

```text
Token
Issuer
Audience
Expiration
Authentication configuration
```

---

# 15. Check Token

Request should contain:

```http
Authorization: Bearer <access-token>
```

Check:

```text
Is token present?
Is it a valid JWT?
Is it expired?
Is it intended for this API?
```

---

# 16. Check Token Expiration

JWT commonly contains:

```text
exp
```

which represents the expiration time.

If the token is expired:

```text
Token
   ↓
Expired
   ↓
401
```

Get a fresh access token and retry.

---

# 17. Check Issuer

JWT contains:

```text
iss
```

The API validates that the token was issued by the expected identity provider/tenant.

For Microsoft Entra ID, your configuration must correspond to the expected tenant/authority.

Conceptually:

```text
Token Issuer
      ↓
Expected Issuer
      ↓
Match?
   ┌──┴──┐
  Yes    No
   ↓      ↓
Continue 401
```

---

# 18. Check Audience

JWT contains:

```text
aud
```

The audience identifies the intended resource/API.

A common problem is:

```text
Token audience
      ≠
API expected audience
```

Then authentication can fail.

Check:

```text
Client configuration
API App Registration
Token
ASP.NET Core JWT configuration
```

---

# 19. Authentication Configuration

Typical ASP.NET Core configuration:

```csharp
builder.Services
    .AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddJwtBearer(options =>
    {
        options.Authority =
            "https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant-id}/v2.0";

        options.Audience = "{api-client-id}";
    });
```

And middleware order:

```csharp
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();

app.MapControllers();
```

A common mistake is:

```csharp
app.UseAuthorization();
app.UseAuthentication();
```

The normal order should be:

```text
Authentication
      ↓
Authorization
      ↓
Endpoints
```

---

